ited president leave both nominations unsettled", "Headline chatter about a blue trend and GOP base cracks is speculative and unquantified"], "rationale": "Utah's eleven-point Republican lean is the only hard evidence available, and recent coverage offers color but no data showing movement; the state stays Likely Republican, with wide uncertainty reflecting the polling vacuum.", "watch": "Any general-election polling, a divisive Republican nomination fight, or a well-funded Democratic nominee could pull the margin toward single digits.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Utah remains one of the most reliably Republican states in presidential politics, with a partisan lean of roughly eleven points toward the GOP. The 2028 race is open, with a term-limited president leaving the field to new nominees on both sides. No public general-election polls, expert ratings, or market prices are available for this race, so the forecast rests on the state's fundamental partisan balance.\n\nRecent coverage shows signs of political churn: Democratic hopefuls are courting Utah activists, opinion writers are asking whether the state could trend blue, and Republican convention fights have exposed some base tensions. But Utah Republicans continue to rate their party's figures more favorably than the president, and nothing in the record quantifies a genuine shift. The state stays in the Likely Republican column, with wide uncertainty given the absence of polling.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1067, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1478, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.1592, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1542, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.1457, "m